Relevant tables / columns and why they matter  
• ratings.avg_rating : to keep only the films whose average rating is above 8  
• ratings.movie_id : key we will join on to every other movie–related table  
• genre.movie_id : connects a film to one (of possibly several) genres  
• genre.genre : the genre name we need to count movies per genre  
• director_mapping.movie_id : links a film to its director(s)  
• director_mapping.name_id : the director identifier to be translated to a person’s name  
• names.id : primary key (matches director_mapping.name_id)  
• names.name : the human‑readable director name we will finally display  

Typical conditions that will be used  
• ratings.avg_rating > 8                     -- only “high‑rated” movies  
• genre.genre IN (<top‑3‑genres‑subquery>)  -- keep only movies in the three busiest genres  
• GROUP BY / ORDER BY with COUNT(*)         -- to rank genres and then directors  

Below are ten exploratory → increasingly complex SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that one could run to understand the data and finally obtain the required answer.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at movies whose average rating is strictly above 8
SELECT movie_id, avg_rating
FROM ratings
WHERE avg_rating > 8
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. See how many such high‑rated films exist in total
SELECT COUNT(*) AS high_rated_movies
FROM ratings
WHERE avg_rating > 8;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect which genres those high‑rated movies belong to (movie‑genre pairs)
SELECT DISTINCT r.movie_id, g.genre
FROM ratings AS r
JOIN genre   AS g ON r.movie_id = g.movie_id
WHERE r.avg_rating > 8
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Count high‑rated movies per genre and order the genres by that count
SELECT g.genre,
       COUNT(*) AS high_rated_movie_cnt
FROM ratings AS r
JOIN genre   AS g ON r.movie_id = g.movie_id
WHERE r.avg_rating > 8
GROUP BY g.genre
ORDER BY high_rated_movie_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Retrieve just the TOP‑3 genres with the most high‑rated movies
SELECT g.genre
FROM ratings AS r
JOIN genre   AS g ON r.movie_id = g.movie_id
WHERE r.avg_rating > 8
GROUP BY g.genre
ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

```sql
-- 6. Show all high‑rated movie IDs that fall into any of those TOP‑3 genres
SELECT DISTINCT r.movie_id
FROM ratings AS r
JOIN genre   AS g ON r.movie_id = g.movie_id
WHERE r.avg_rating > 8
  AND g.genre IN (
        SELECT g2.genre
        FROM ratings AS r2
        JOIN genre   AS g2 ON r2.movie_id = g2.movie_id
        WHERE r2.avg_rating > 8
        GROUP BY g2.genre
        ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
        LIMIT 3
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Look at which directors worked on those qualifying movies
SELECT DISTINCT dm.name_id, dm.movie_id
FROM director_mapping AS dm
JOIN (
        SELECT DISTINCT r.movie_id
        FROM ratings AS r
        JOIN genre   AS g ON r.movie_id = g.movie_id
        WHERE r.avg_rating > 8
          AND g.genre IN (
                SELECT g2.genre
                FROM ratings AS r2
                JOIN genre   AS g2 ON r2.movie_id = g2.movie_id
                WHERE r2.avg_rating > 8
                GROUP BY g2.genre
                ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
                LIMIT 3
          )
     ) AS sub  ON dm.movie_id = sub.movie_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Translate those director IDs to names
SELECT DISTINCT n.id   AS director_id,
                n.name AS director_name
FROM names AS n
JOIN director_mapping AS dm ON n.id = dm.name_id
JOIN (
        SELECT DISTINCT r.movie_id
        FROM ratings AS r
        JOIN genre   AS g ON r.movie_id = g.movie_id
        WHERE r.avg_rating > 8
          AND g.genre IN (
                SELECT g2.genre
                FROM ratings AS r2
                JOIN genre   AS g2 ON r2.movie_id = g2.movie_id
                WHERE r2.avg_rating > 8
                GROUP BY g2.genre
                ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
                LIMIT 3
          )
     ) AS sub  ON dm.movie_id = sub.movie_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Count how many qualifying high‑rated films each director has in those genres
SELECT n.name                             AS director_name,
       COUNT(DISTINCT dm.movie_id)        AS high_rated_movie_cnt
FROM names             AS n
JOIN director_mapping  AS dm ON n.id = dm.name_id
JOIN ratings           AS r  ON dm.movie_id = r.movie_id
JOIN genre             AS g  ON r.movie_id = g.movie_id
WHERE r.avg_rating > 8
  AND g.genre IN (
        SELECT g2.genre
        FROM ratings AS r2
        JOIN genre   AS g2 ON r2.movie_id = g2.movie_id
        WHERE r2.avg_rating > 8
        GROUP BY g2.genre
        ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
        LIMIT 3
  )
GROUP BY n.name
ORDER BY high_rated_movie_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL QUERY: top‑4 directors with the most high‑rated (>8) movies
--                    inside the three busiest genres
SELECT n.name                             AS director_name,
       COUNT(DISTINCT dm.movie_id)        AS high_rated_movie_cnt
FROM names             AS n
JOIN director_mapping  AS dm ON n.id = dm.name_id
JOIN ratings           AS r  ON dm.movie_id = r.movie_id
JOIN genre             AS g  ON r.movie_id = g.movie_id
WHERE r.avg_rating > 8
  AND g.genre IN (
        -- subquery to obtain the three genres that have the largest number
        -- of movies with avg_rating > 8
        SELECT g2.genre
        FROM ratings AS r2
        JOIN genre   AS g2 ON r2.movie_id = g2.movie_id
        WHERE r2.avg_rating > 8
        GROUP BY g2.genre
        ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
        LIMIT 3
  )
GROUP BY n.name
ORDER BY high_rated_movie_cnt DESC
LIMIT 4;
```

Each query is standalone, uses only the provided tables, avoids schema/introspection statements, employs DISTINCT when helpful, and caps its output to 20 rows.
